As far as what pages or functionality should be protected COOP is not just for outgoing links/navigation but can also mitigate XS-Leak attacks like Frame Counting (which will not be impacted by the use of `noopener`). Here is an example where the target page is opened by the malicious app:

```js
// some-malicious-site.com

// create a new window
var target = 'https://www.target_site.com/vulnerable_page';
var w = window.open(target);

// wait 2s for page load
setTimeout(() => {
  // Log the number of frames on the target page, revealing some information
  console.log("The page has %d iframes loaded", w.length);
}, 200);
```

Though this is a distinct attack from tabnabbing, we can conceptualize it as a single problem to mitigate:

> Prevent malicious applications from sharing access to a Window object

If we try to make a precise conditional based on application functionality that could be impacted, it could get complex.
